<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap letter">
    <p class="prompt">git log --reverse | head -1</p>
    <h2>Built inside a monorepo that runs a dozen services.</h2>
    <p>I work at a startup on a monorepo with two apps, each with its own frontend, backend and back office, and a Docker setup running several databases and services. As AI agents took on more of the work, we could run several tasks at once. Every task then needed its own running stack, so that I could check what an agent had done, and so that the agent could check it itself.</p>
    <p>It started as shell scripts: one to create a worktree, copy its <code>.env</code> and run the hooks, another to clean it up. Rewriting the ports inside the <code>.env</code> files by hand soon became the hard part. I carried those scripts to my own projects, and tried the tools built around creating worktrees. They stopped at the worktree, and none of them could run a stack like ours.</p>
    <p>So I built wtm. Today the whole team uses it every day at work, to run tasks in several worktrees at once and to start part or all of the monorepo on its own ports in each.</p>
    <div class="signature">
      <img src="https://github.com/LucasPcq.png?size=96" alt="" width="48" height="48" loading="lazy" />
